{
  "pluginId": "com.digitalarsenal.flows.terrain-ingest",
  "name": "Terrain Pyramid Ingest Flow",
  "version": "0.2.0",
  "pluginFamily": "flow",
  "sequentialJustification": {
    "kind": "caller-level-parallelism",
    "detail": "Every linked guest is wasi-sequential; the flow dispatches them serially and delegates external concurrency to its host."
  },
  "description": "OFF-FLEET pyramid builder for the quantized-mesh terrain tileset. One timer tick is ONE GRANULE CELL of work. mark_query asks hostcap/flatsql-query (one $FSO QUERY_RECORDS) for the durable resume mark - an $IRM record, through the code-named FlatSQL view (SELECT _data FROM IRM ORDER BY _rowid DESC LIMIT ?) - and the answer comes back as $FSB chunks (the query's $FSO status lands on egress); granule_plan resolves the next cell of a REGION-PRIORITY enumeration that is a pure function of the config, and emits EIGHT HttpRequests - the 2x2 granule neighbourhood in elevation and water-body form, each body arriving verbatim in its HttpResponse (no base64) - plus ONE plan ($DTT header records, one per tile of that level whose SOUTH-WEST CORNER the cell holds), the encoder's options (ModuleConfig: maxGridSize, skipOceanTiles) and the job (the $IRM the cell will make durable). terrain-source:tile decodes that granule set ONCE, windowed to the union extent under a 256 MiB in-guest budget, and emits one $DTT record per tile with the water mask classified on the level's global post lattice; all-ocean tiles are NOT stored (they are identical, there are millions, and the serving flow synthesizes an unstored address inside published availability as exactly that). ingest_meta authors the IngestRequest the storage lane cannot invent - dataset, batch, epoch - carrying the tile report's record count (a FlowNotice COUNT) to catch a batch that reports success and stores nothing, and the primary granule response's ETag; hostcap/storage-ingest appends the batch ($DTT on dtt_records, RECONCILE None); publish_request advances the resume mark FROM THE VERIFIED STORE RESULT, never at dispatch, so a crash between fetch and store re-runs the cell instead of leaving an invisible hole; the mark is the job's $IRM completed from the IngestResult, persisted through hostcap/storage-write (record; there is no meta port - the type is the frame's own) under the storage_write grant, and the same $IRM lands on egress for an operator to read. A batch storage REFUSED stops the run (ingest-refused) without moving the mark. Before that, nothing wrote the mark at all: it went only to egress, the flow declared no storage-write capability, and the read query named a table no node has - so every tick restarted at cell 0, silently. NO PUBSUB ANNOUNCE IN V1 (Themis: a tileset-epoch announcement would be a SIGNED record and that contract is not cut). publish_request's optional announce request output is deliberately left UNWIRED rather than pointed at a placeholder; the $DTT records reach the fleet through the existing dataset-publication lane. Levels shallower than 8 are not built here: a tile wider than a granule breaks the containment the 2x2 neighbourhood rests on, and those levels are built by DOWNSAMPLING deeper tiles over the record store. granule_plan refuses them by name. PLACEMENT: this flow runs OFF-FLEET on the build machine under Docker linux/amd64 WasmEdge, the same law binaries are built under - never on host-01 or host-02, whose workload placement is the owner's. TYPED CONTRACTS 1.0: every frame between nodes is a FlatBuffer - TimerTick, $FSO/$FSB, HttpRequest/HttpResponse, $DTT, ModuleConfig, FlowNotice, $IRM, IngestRequest/IngestResult, $PNM - no JSON and no base64.",
  "capabilities": [
    "clock",
    "http",
    "storage_ingest",
    "storage_query",
    "storage_write"
  ],
